  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 228 AM CDT Wed Oct 28 2026\n\nUpper level ridging over the Quad State will slightly break to\nour northwest as a disturbance moves from Kansas City towards\nChicago. Rainfall tracks well northwest of the Quad State. With\nlarge surface high pressure to our east, a familiar pattern from\nthe last several days is projected for today and tomorrow: dry \nconditions, above normal temperatures, light winds, and \nlocalized patchy morning fog. \n\nThursday night, a trailing cold front extending from a Canadian\nsystem may brush the northern portions of the Quad State.\nPrecipitation is not expected, nor is any notable drop in\ntemperatures. As we move into the weekend, the remnants of\nTropical Storm Vance are projected to track northeastward\nthrough Texas. There remains some model variation in timing and\npositioning, but PoPs Saturday into Saturday evening have \ndecreased some as models are dialing in focus a little bit \nlater. Can't rule out rain interfering with Halloween activities\nquite yet, with evening PoPs of 30-40% in SEMO (though, \nconsidering these are 6 hr PoPs, early evening chances would be\na bit lower). A negatively tilted shortwave trough moves \nthrough Sunday morning, accompanying some of the better precip \nchances with the weekend system. Overall QPF is still limited \nwith amounts maxing out near an inch in the Southern Pennyrile, \nbut PWs near 200% of normal can put higher amounts in play. NBM \n48hr QPF probs hold at 30% chance of an inch or more in West \nKentucky and 5-10% of two inches. Convective potential has \nshifted down again, with thunder removed from the blend. With \nthe arrival of this system, and afterwards, highs drop from \naround 80 to closer to 70, though lows remain well above normal \nin the 50s.\n\nTwo systems arrive at the West Coast over the weekend, with low\npressure dropping southeastward to the Pacific Northwest, and\nanother system moving east-northeastward into Southern\nCalifornia. Split flow merges over the Rockies and develops into\na large system in the Plains, though perhaps not as extensive a\nspiral as the deterministic GFS. A steady stream of moisture \npulls from the Gulf into the system, and an amplifying pattern \nwith ridges over the Rockies and east coast can stall out the \nsystem over the Plains through midweek. Ensemble mean 48hr \nrainfall over the Quad State with this system is 1-1.5\", while \nthe deterministic is higher. Some portions of the \nPlains/Mississippi River Valley could receive several inches of \nrain, but it remains to be seen whether the Quad State will end \nup in the rainier zone. After all, multiple primary components \nof this system are days from the West Coast.",
